WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think we’ve got everybody
who should be here is here. We’ve got no

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.620
apologies for absence. Delighted that Dido
is with us for the whole meeting. Declarations

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:22.390
of interest anybody? Any comment on the minutes
of the last board meeting? Right, well just

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.060
a very brief – briefly from Dido and myself.
Dido, do you want to say a few words.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.919
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Well no, just how great it
is to be with everybody but just very briefly

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:38.890
to remind everyone that this is a board meeting
in public, not a public meeting and obviously

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.359
given the circumstances as we’re still in
a level 4 incident, we’re conducting this

00:00:43.359 --> 00:00:48.980
entirely virtually. What that does mean, though,
is I’m sure David you’ll join me, if anybody

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.609
does want to raise issues or questions with
David and I after the meeting directly, we’re

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:58.679
always available and happy to do that but
not in the meeting itself, as normal.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.380
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you Dido. And I think
just from Dido and myself, you know, this

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.620
is our first – you know, this is – we’re
having this board meeting when we still have

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:17.220
a really serious health crisis in the country
and I think again from both of us just to

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:22.190
recognise the extraordinary work that’s
being done across the NHS has been done over

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:27.560
the last three months will have to carry on
being done over the next few months. So with

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.790
that I’m going to hand over to Simon.
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Hear, hear.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.920
&gt;&gt;David Prior: To give his Chief Executive
Report.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.900
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well thank you David, thank
you colleagues. We last met on 26th March

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:47.500
as a public board meeting and on that day
there were just over 5,000 coronavirus positive

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.990
in patients in hospitals across England. A
fortnight later, that number reached nearly

00:01:53.990 --> 00:02:01.770
19,000 and the number of coronavirus patients
in critical care tripled. So the NHS has been

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:09.360
under enormous pressure in the intervening
days and weeks since the middle and end of

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:18.200
March. We’ve looked after 102,000 coronavirus
positive in patients, most of whom elderly,

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:24.400
all of whom vulnerable and every single person
who needed hospital care in this country for

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:32.459
coronavirus able to get it. That is at its
heart a tribute to the work of NHS staff right

00:02:32.459 --> 00:02:37.450
across the country and the very first thing
we must do as a board, I think, is join the

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:44.100
whole of the rest of the country in placing
on record our gratitude to our colleagues.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:54.139
Next weekend will mark the 72nd anniversary
of the NHS and on Saturday 4th July we will

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:59.340
take time to remember and mourn those that
we and families and communities have lost

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:07.519
to coronavirus through this pandemic. And
then the next day on Sunday 5th, we will once

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:14.140
again [inaudible] carers but this time it
will be those of us in the NHS who are paying

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:22.180
tribute and thanking everybody who helped
us help patients across the country.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:28.120
We have a massive job on our hands now to
ensure that the health service is there for

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.780
the full range of conditions that we are set
up to treat while doing so in a continuing

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:40.980
era of the threat of coronavirus. A big part
of what we’re going to be discussing today

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:46.030
and with the health service over the coming
several weeks is how we restart those services

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:52.109
that inevitably have to be deferred while
we were looking after this huge spike in coronavirus

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:59.180
patients. And Amanda and I will be setting
out for the NHS the so-called phase three

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:07.349
of the response in early July.
Since January 30th, we have been operating

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:12.870
at a so-called level 4 national incident as
part of the emergency preparedness and response

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:19.020
framework. This is the longest sustained national
emergency response that the health service

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:26.930
has had to deal with in its 72 year history.
My intention is that we will see during the

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:36.389
course of July the unfolding of some of the
social restrictions and the impact that that

00:04:36.389 --> 00:04:43.699
has on the infection rate and assuming that
that continues to succeed and we don’t see

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:49.340
significant upward spikes in that period then
come the end of July we would look to move

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:55.920
down from being a level 4 national incident
and we would set that out by July 31st. But

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:03.699
we want to stay highly attentive during July
to how the pandemic continues to evolve before

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:08.030
making that call.
In the meantime, it’s also worth of course

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:13.110
recording that lots of other aspects of the
health services work has continued. I’d

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:19.940
like to thank GPs and the fact that over 98%
of them have now joined the new primary care

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:27.700
network and the PCN contracts that were on
offer. I’d like to thank trusts as well

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:34.830
as CCGs across the country for the financial
discipline that was shown throughout last

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:42.550
year, 2019-20. We’ll be laying our combined
accounts before Parliament in due course but

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:49.090
as the audit process comes to a conclusion,
it is clear that once again the NHS balanced

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:55.919
its books and as planned we halved the number
of trusts in deficit while also delivering

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:02.009
on important commitments such as the mental
health investment standard by which we guarantee

00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:07.650
that at a national level the resources going
into mental health services grow faster than

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:14.830
those for the NHS overall. But the key challenge
in front of us now is to ensure that as we

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:21.560
restart services we lock in some of the innovative
gains that we have made during this period

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:28.650
and we use the harsh spotlight that coronavirus
has shone on deep-seated health and social

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:33.020
inequalities in this country, particularly
as they relate to black, Asian and minority

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:39.990
ethnic communities but not only those members
of our society to redesign a proactive response

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:44.300
as to what a new NHS will look like. And that’s
why we make no apology for having that as

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:49.410
a further specific agenda item before us this
afternoon. Thank you very much, David.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:56.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much Simon.
So any questions from the Board to Simon?

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.460
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: [Inaudible].
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No questions from Simon. That’s

00:07:11.469 --> 00:07:13.482
unusual. You’re going to get away with chiefly
today.

00:07:13.482 --> 00:07:16.240
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I think they’re just gaging
for the next item, that’s the key point.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.659
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well exactly, yeah. Well should
we move on then to the next item. And I think

00:07:22.659 --> 00:07:29.479
Pauline Philip has joined us. I can see Pauline
somewhere, who’s joining Amanda and Julian.

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:34.840
Amanda, are you going to kick off on this?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Yes, thank you very much,

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:41.080
David. I will if I may. So the paper that
the Board has in front of them is a report

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:48.420
on our ongoing COVID response and NHS recovery.
As Simon says, we’ve over the course of

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.540
the last three months looked after – well
actually, it’s an update to the board paper

00:07:52.540 --> 00:08:00.300
because it is now 102,000 rather than just
over 100,000 COVID-19 positive patients and

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:05.849
I think I just want to add my thanks to his,
and I know this has been said many times and

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:11.419
will be said many more, just what an extraordinary
response we have seen across the NHS to a

00:08:11.419 --> 00:08:16.389
period of completely unprecedented scale of
challenge.

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:22.780
Two things worth saying beyond that is obviously
what we have seen is a significant disruption

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:28.470
to the kind of NHS business as usual over
the last few months and we’ve also seen

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:34.919
a very significant impact on the behaviour
of the public in how they have accessed health

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:39.110
services over that time.
So in the paper, the first thing that we’ve

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:43.979
talked about here, and I’m sure Pauline
will want to say more about this, is around

00:08:43.979 --> 00:08:48.920
urgent and emergency care where what we’ve
seen over the last few months actually has

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:53.980
been a very significant drop off in the number
of people attending accident and emergency

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:58.680
in particular. And although attendances are
now rising again, they’re not yet back to

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:06.589
pre-COVID levels. Encouragingly, it’s clear
that the vast majority of patients who didn’t

00:09:06.589 --> 00:09:12.670
go to Accident Emergency over this time would
fall into what we would recognise as the minor

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:19.860
category of illness rather than the major
category and those areas where people are

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:26.170
sicker and need urgent care, like, for example,
cardiac. Those levels have returned now to

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:31.690
pre-COVID levels. Another area where we’ve
seen, actually, a real change in behaviour

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:41.010
has been access to 111. So the 111 service
increased calls from around 300 to 350,000

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:48.200
per week to 800,000 per week during March.
And again, activity levels have gone back

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:53.620
to much more like normal levels but clearly
there is an opportunity for us to continue

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:58.860
to direct people to 111 because that’s a
very effective way of making sure the public

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:07.410
are directed to the right place for care rather
than necessarily always requiring face to

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:12.690
face care.
We’ve seen just – if I just move on to

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:22.670
talking about referrals treatment, times and
access, what we did do during the COVID response

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:30.590
was ask the NHS to prioritise urgent surgery
and urgent access. So particularly, for example,

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:41.120
cancer care and therefore to cut back on non-urgent
elective activity. At the same time, the number

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:47.980
of people being referred into the system for
elective care also decreased. So overall,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:53.201
actually, the total waiting list size fell
across March and April. But I think absolutely

00:10:53.201 --> 00:10:58.670
what we have seen and we would expect to see
is an increase in the length of time than

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:05.040
some of the less urgent people – peoples
whose requirements are less urgent are now

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:11.190
waiting. We’ve seen at the same time a big
increase in the use of digital, telephone

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:16.279
and video consultations to ensure that access
has been maintained for people of primary

00:11:16.279 --> 00:11:21.040
care, mental health and secondary care over
this period and that again is something that

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:27.480
we’re clearly seeing continued and maintained
as we move into this kind of next phase of

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:32.680
the COVID response.
I’ve just mentioned cancer and just to say

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:38.160
one final thing on cancer, which is that we
have seen again over recent weeks an increase

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.329
in the number of people being referred on
our two week wait cancer pathways. I think

00:11:43.329 --> 00:11:49.610
we would still want very much to emphasise
the message that anyone with worrying symptoms

00:11:49.610 --> 00:12:00.519
should seek help from primary care and that
we are absolutely open for business as far

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:06.000
as cancer care is concerned. We have introduced
a national campaign ‘Help Us To Help You’,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.529
which I hope and we believe has gone some
way to reassuring people that actually they

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:15.440
should still come forward if they have worrying
symptoms.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:21.060
Final just few things to mention, I just wanted
to say a word about diagnostics because we

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:29.970
are very aware that some of the challenges
of providing care in a safe way when you have

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:38.070
coronavirus circulating require us to operate
now with infection control procedures PPE

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:47.120
that do make it much more difficult to provide
some services as efficiently and as productively

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:53.230
as we were able to do in a kind of non-COVID
situation. Diagnostics has been an area that

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:57.750
struggle particularly with that because it’s
such a generally high throughput area that

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:04.300
adding time in for cleaning, for donning and
doffing PPE, for air changes does make a substantial

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:10.649
impact on the ability to get through a high
volume set of patients. So we have seen an

00:13:10.649 --> 00:13:15.290
increase in the number of people waiting over
six weeks for diagnostic tests. It’s an

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:21.660
area where local systems are working very
hard at the moment to come up with plans to

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:27.899
make sure that they are maximising the available
capacity that they have and thinking about

00:13:27.899 --> 00:13:32.550
new pathways, new ways of providing services
efficiently and effectively and also looking

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:36.980
at other options such as use of the private
sector, independent sector to provide additional

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:40.850
capacity.
Final couple of things, just to say to echo

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:45.579
what Simon has said about again the extraordinary
response from colleagues in primary care,

00:13:45.579 --> 00:13:51.880
community care, mental health, community pharmacy,
dental, optometry. We’ve put in place a

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:59.710
remote triage model in primary care and in
fact in all of those areas we’ve seen the

00:13:59.710 --> 00:14:04.660
creation of new services such as the pharmacy
clinical assessment service, the recruitment

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:12.639
of over 1,600 GPs for the COVID clinical assessment
service. All have made a huge contribution

00:14:12.639 --> 00:14:19.680
to maintaining access to services through
this COVID period. And dental services, similarly,

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:27.569
over 800,000 remote triages have taken place
over the past few months and urgent dental

00:14:27.569 --> 00:14:33.670
care, of course, provided within 628 community,
primary and secondary care settings as part

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:39.911
of our urgent dental care hubs.
Mental health has continued throughout. They

00:14:39.911 --> 00:14:44.350
have been able to go faster on implementing
some of the commitments in the long term plan,

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:54.540
such as 24/7 crisis services and they continue
to see the same commitment we have continued

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.339
maintain this – the same commitment – sorry,
there was just a bit of – just heard a noise

00:14:59.339 --> 00:15:06.290
on the line. They’ve continued to maintain
the same funding commitments. So mental health,

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:11.040
absolutely maintained throughout and gone
further, gone faster indeed as many areas

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:17.350
have in response to the challenge.
Final thing I just wanted to say in terms

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:23.279
of service specific update was around people
with a learning disability, autism or both.

00:15:23.279 --> 00:15:28.220
During the pandemic we have seen an increase
in discharges from specialists in patient

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:34.220
care, which is very much what we had planned
for pre-COVID as well, but a huge amount of

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:39.069
work underway now of course to ensure that
we have the community support available to

00:15:39.069 --> 00:15:44.009
mean people can be supported effectively in
the community and that we don’t now see

00:15:44.009 --> 00:15:49.389
an increase in admissions given that we are
expecting to see an increase in demand for

00:15:49.389 --> 00:15:56.319
mental health services on the back of COVID-19.
Similar level of investment obviously maintained

00:15:56.319 --> 00:16:02.069
to support learning disabilities and/or autism
services going forward.

00:16:02.069 --> 00:16:07.160
Final thing from me is just, so what’s next.
We’re working now with all of the regions

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:12.779
who are in turn working with systems to make
sure that we’ve got clear restoration plans

00:16:12.779 --> 00:16:17.790
in place across all of these services. At
a national level, we’ve maintained, as Simon

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:22.930
said, under our level 4 incident our cell
structure so that we’re providing support

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:29.360
across a number of key areas: workforce, policy
areas, really being able to continue to use

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:34.050
the expertise of our national clinic leads
to design and develop and then implement best

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:41.230
practice pathways, continue to work with partners
in public health on implementation of infection

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:45.980
control – infection, prevention and control
measures, continue to work with colleagues

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:51.140
in the department of health on things like
the supply of PPE. So we’re continuing to

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:56.339
keep that structure in place nationally to
support regions and systems to deliver their

00:16:56.339 --> 00:17:03.339
restoration plans. And as Simon says, that
provides, then, I hope a very clear and strong

00:17:03.339 --> 00:17:10.689
platform from which we will then be able to
move on to our phase 3 recovery plans across

00:17:10.689 --> 00:17:17.650
summer and autumn.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Amanda.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:22.569
Pauline, do you have anything you want to
add on to what Amanda has said?

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:28.209
&gt;&gt;Pauline Geen: I have very little to add
and I think Amanda has covered most of the

00:17:28.209 --> 00:17:33.990
paper. Maybe just one or two small points
to make. I think in addition to the excellent

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:40.110
work that was done by our 111 service during
the period to just call out the work that

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:46.010
has also been done by our ambulance services
and I think the Board will note from the paper

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:54.140
that during the month of May the ambulance
service met all six standards nationally.

00:17:54.140 --> 00:18:01.080
So I think that that really needs to be acknowledged
and I think also during the peak of the COVID

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:07.169
period, the excellent work that they did around
non-conveyancing, i.e., going patients care

00:18:07.169 --> 00:18:14.420
at home, see and treat, hear and treat, etc.
On the elective side, I think probably the

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:22.770
only point that I could add is that we absolutely
acknowledge that was the size of the waiting

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:29.700
list has come down, that we do have an increasing
problem around our long waits and as part

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:36.230
of the restoration work that Amanda referred
to, there is a major focus on understanding

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:41.809
every patient on the waiting list but clearly
focussing on patients who have been waiting

00:18:41.809 --> 00:18:48.940
for long periods. And then I think maybe the
final thing is to just highlight and again

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:55.520
acknowledge the excellent work that’s been
done on the out-patient redesign and in particular

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:02.440
the fact that 80% of our trusts now have access
to video consultations. I think we’ve had

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:07.440
something like a quarter of a million video
consultations and outpatients since the beginning

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:11.299
of the year. So I think that’s all I can
add, really.

00:19:11.299 --> 00:19:15.710
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Pauline.
I wonder, Ruth, if you can just give a flavour

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:22.450
of what – the effects of control measures
we’re taking, how that really impacts upon

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.799
sort of frontline nursing.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, Chairman.

00:19:26.799 --> 00:19:33.060
I’m really proud of how the nurses and midwives
and all of our care staff have really pulled

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:39.429
together, responded to this COVID-19. It makes
me immensely proud as Chief Nursing Officer

00:19:39.429 --> 00:19:47.510
but also when I’m stood beside them working
a shift and I’ve done several shifts in

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:55.890
all sorts of settings during this period and
everyone working as a team has made that possible.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:20:00.190
We know that in temperatures like we have
today and we’ve seen very warm temperatures

00:20:00.190 --> 00:20:08.650
over the last few months that PPE is difficult
to wear. It’s hot, sweaty work and challenging

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:16.299
and emotionally challenging times as well.
So infection prevention control is led by

00:20:16.299 --> 00:20:20.559
lots of people on the frontline and there’s
always a director of infection prevention

00:20:20.559 --> 00:20:28.070
and control that can be a doctor or a nurse
leading that at every organisation. They have

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:35.970
had guidance across from the UK, guidance
that has been developed and published but

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:42.900
most of all it’s been the frontline staff
working to make sure that they are looking

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:47.820
after patients where they have COVID and if
they don’t have COVID, making sure that

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:53.789
they look after that too. We’ve also been
issued recently some guidance around face

00:20:53.789 --> 00:21:02.110
masks in hospitals so that all staff are wearing
that where they’re in contact with hospital

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:06.250
corridors, for example, really important we
do that.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:14.200
So I’d just like to say that my overwhelming
experience during this last few months is

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:23.240
huge pride and people have worked so hard
to protect their patients and to protect their

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:29.381
selves as a member of their profession but
they’ve also worked amazingly well as a

00:21:29.381 --> 00:21:35.809
team. So hats off to nurses and midwives and
care workers across the whole of our NHS and

00:21:35.809 --> 00:21:39.070
care.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much Ruth. And

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:45.440
Steve from the doctors’ perspective it’s
a very difficult balance between infection

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:53.080
control and wanting to try and get through
the diagnostic and elective care waiting times.

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:57.929
How is the profession handling that difficult
dynamic?

00:21:57.929 --> 00:22:04.150
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: So thank you, David. And
firstly, I’d like to, as others have done,

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:09.900
pay tribute to the tremendous work of my colleagues,
obviously particularly doctors but not just

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:15.929
doctors but all staff clinical and non-clinical,
everything that they have done over the time

00:22:15.929 --> 00:22:21.270
since our last board meeting in maintaining
service and providing the response we’ve

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:25.620
managed to provide to COVID. And I think as
Simon says, the important thing is that every

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:31.440
patient who’s required treatment with COVID-19
during the surge we saw in March/April and

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:37.600
into May has had that treatment.
But you’re right, we’re now in that transition

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:45.409
period where we’re moving from a peak of
infections in April down to a level of lower

00:22:45.409 --> 00:22:51.029
community transmission, lower prevalence in
the community and as Simon said earlier, we

00:22:51.029 --> 00:22:56.070
certainly hope that the steady relaxation
of measures, that low prevalence in the community

00:22:56.070 --> 00:23:01.890
will be continued. But there’s no doubt
we would continue for some months yet to have

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:07.150
to manage patients with COVID-19, we still
have several thousand patients in our beds

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:13.120
in England who are – colleagues are treating
with COVID-19. So that is decreasing but decreasing

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:19.350
slowly. And we also have to face this balance
between protecting staff and patients through

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:24.190
good infection prevention and control with
a new virus, a virus that we haven’t had

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:29.039
to deal with before with the requirement and
the need to get back to business, as Amanda

00:23:29.039 --> 00:23:35.799
has said. And there is no doubt that there
is a tension, if you want, there is a balance

00:23:35.799 --> 00:23:41.679
to be struck between that good infection prevention
and control which is paramount but also getting

00:23:41.679 --> 00:23:47.970
that sort of throughout put in terms of cases
through that we’re used to in all our services,

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:54.490
from diagnostic services through to emergency
service and routine services. And I think

00:23:54.490 --> 00:24:00.490
at this point, as we’re transiting from
the peak through to lower prevalence in the

00:24:00.490 --> 00:24:04.419
communities, we are learning how to do that
and that will continue to evolve.

00:24:04.419 --> 00:24:09.580
The final point I – the final two points
I’d make is one, it is challenging but we

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:14.710
will be working our way through with colleagues
and secondly it’s not unique to us, so you

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:19.659
and I had spoken to colleagues in other countries
recently, David, and they have described the

00:24:19.659 --> 00:24:24.510
challenges they’ve having in doing exactly
the same. And I know Tim Ferriss is one of

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:29.830
our non-executive directors who I think should
be on the call today. I can’t see him on

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:34.250
my iPad but I’m sure he’s there. He’s
– works in the Massachusetts General Hospital

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:39.149
in Boston, very famous teaching hospital,
and he would describe to exactly the same

00:24:39.149 --> 00:24:44.210
challenges that they face in that healthcare
environment. So I think collectively we need

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:50.450
to work our way through how we manage COVID
in this new environment, maintaining infection

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:54.870
prevention and control to keep staff and patients
safe, to give patients confidence but also

00:24:54.870 --> 00:25:00.880
to ensure we get services back up and running
so that we can provide the care at the speed

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:05.559
that we’ve always provided to the citizens
of this country.

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:09.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much Steve.
I mean, Tim, could you give us a perspective

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:16.020
from Boston, if you like, and how different
is it there compared to where it is here?

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:23.120
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferriss: Sure David. It’s actually
remarkably similar. And I should start off

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:31.370
by following all of my colleagues on the Board
in congratulating the US in their accomplishments.

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:38.470
I have done shifts myself in our ICUs and
on our floors and in our respiratory out patient

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:47.680
facilities and understand the tension between
all the PPE and the fears which have largely

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:55.149
abated because we have shown that PPE is effective.
But then, moving more directly to your question,

00:25:55.149 --> 00:26:05.679
David and following up on what Steve says,
the backlog of services and the need to produce

00:26:05.679 --> 00:26:12.830
high quality services for a large number of
people who’ve really had delayed services

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:20.659
and the population health impacts of those
delays, we need to really focus on how we

00:26:20.659 --> 00:26:28.020
get back to work but in a way that acknowledges
the safety issues around working in a world

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:37.630
where we don’t have and will not have perfect
knowledge of who is infected. And so, balancing

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:45.280
the risks and the production issues is – comes
right down to the point of the people working

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:52.080
on the frontlines. And I personally take an
improvement framework on this, which is we

00:26:52.080 --> 00:27:00.919
have to make policies that balance these various
competing goods and then rapidly learn and

00:27:00.919 --> 00:27:11.659
improve as we learn more. So I’ve been very
impressed with the work done in the NHS to

00:27:11.659 --> 00:27:20.860
date and look forward to being of any help
that I can in navigating at the point of care

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:26.679
delivery how exactly we manage the safety
and production issues.

00:27:26.679 --> 00:27:32.580
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Tim. But I wonder
Ara, you’ve – just from the role NHS have

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:38.730
played in research over this time and clinical
trials and thinking about the vaccine at Imperial

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:51.630
and Oxford and recovery trials, have you got
any comments you’d like to make? Steve,

00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:53.800
maybe you’d like to pick up that question.
I can’t see – Ara’s slipped off the

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.400
screen for a minute.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, I’m very happy to

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:02.101
pick that up. So I’ll start with the recovery
trial, which has been going on in over 170

00:28:02.101 --> 00:28:09.580
of our trusts throughout the country, it’s
a UK wide study, and I think what that shows

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:16.450
is the real benefit of having a coordinated
joined up health system where we can really

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:23.130
pursue research questions at PACE. And the
trialists at Oxford University who’ve been

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:29.850
leading this are very well used to designing
simple studies that can be done in this sort

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:35.770
of situation. They’ve got a long history
of this sort of study with a fairly straight

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:42.460
forward design that can be done at a very
busy time but get the answers that are required.

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:51.899
And I think it’s a tribute to that group
but also to the research capabilities and

00:28:51.899 --> 00:28:56.409
the research intent of this country to get
a question through research and of course

00:28:56.409 --> 00:29:05.309
the determination of our colleagues in our
NHS hospitals and organisations to participate

00:29:05.309 --> 00:29:09.920
and to ensure that patients are offered the
chance to join these studies to get that answer.

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:15.200
And of course, above all to the patients who
agreed and consented to go into the study.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:21.480
And I think the prepublication has now been
published this week so people can see the

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:28.360
data. It looks impressive, impressive enough
for us to recommend that it’s been taken

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:33.170
into consideration by clinicians and treatment
in the UK. It’s been picked up, I know,

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:36.910
by the World Health Organisation and others
and so I think that is something that the

00:29:36.910 --> 00:29:43.500
NHS should be able to do well, can do well
and has done well during this epidemic.

00:29:43.500 --> 00:29:49.240
Vaccine development, of course, similar story.
We’re going at pace. Lots of vaccine development

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:54.690
across the world. As the Chief Scientific
Advisor and Chief Medical Officer said, vaccine

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:58.260
development is never straightforward. Some
of the vaccines that are being developed are

00:29:58.260 --> 00:30:06.809
R&amp;A vaccines, so those are new types of vaccines.
So again, innovation is at the fore here but

00:30:06.809 --> 00:30:11.510
that, of course, means that there’ll be
additional challenges, no doubt, in getting

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:17.649
those vaccines through. So I think vaccine
development in this epidemic is proceeding

00:30:17.649 --> 00:30:22.010
at a pace that we’ve never seen before.
We are all very hopeful, of course, that we

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:29.049
will get to a vaccine that works as soon as
possible because that is undoubtedly with

00:30:29.049 --> 00:30:34.440
therapeutics, which is what we’ve seen with
dexamethasone in the recovery trial, are the

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:38.890
exit strategies, the way we can get out of
this beyond social distancing.

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:44.460
And the one other thing I would say is it’s
not only in the therapeutics that we can apply

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:50.559
principles of academic study and research.
So for instance, in testing, in knowing who

00:30:50.559 --> 00:30:56.340
to test and when to test in what a positive
antibody test means in terms of immunity,

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:00.299
that is research we can do in the NHS as well.
And Public Health England have launched the

00:31:00.299 --> 00:31:08.340
SIREN study which is a study recruiting 100,000
NHS staff to sequentially measure antigen

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:09.870
response either when you have virus or not
and antibody response, either whether you

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:15.210
have the virus or not, and antibody response
whether you’ve had it and then follow people

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:20.179
over time to determine what immunity they
developed and how long lasting it is. And

00:31:20.179 --> 00:31:24.539
that’s another research question to a slightly
different question but one that’s equally

00:31:24.539 --> 00:31:31.789
important that I think the NHS is incredibly
well placed to answer. And that’s why when

00:31:31.789 --> 00:31:36.559
we wrote out yesterday to trusts, we emphasised
on the advice of Chief Medical officer, that

00:31:36.559 --> 00:31:41.340
we all agree with that that we should be really
be prioritising getting into that sort of

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:45.919
study, getting into SIREN study in particular,
so that we have the answers to the questions

00:31:45.919 --> 00:31:50.820
that remain unanswered at the moment.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you Steve. Any questions

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:55.860
from any of the Board?
&gt;&gt;Speaker: David, just briefly from me, if

00:31:55.860 --> 00:32:03.530
that’s okay. I just also would obviously
want to put my voice on the record in saying

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:12.370
how humbling it has been to watch the extraordinary
work of NHS staff across the country and also

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:18.861
our teams within NHS England and NHS Improvement
and how brilliantly you have all done. My

00:32:18.861 --> 00:32:23.269
questions around the support we’ve been
given our people, and I just wondered whether

00:32:23.269 --> 00:32:27.549
Amanda and I think Prerana is on the Board
call, whether you could talk a little bit

00:32:27.549 --> 00:32:34.710
to the support that we have provided across
the country to our NHS staff in all roles

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:38.890
and also what we’re doing going forward
to support them in the journey of restoring

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:43.380
and sort of recovering services.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Prerana.

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:54.910
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Sorry, I was waiting for
Amanda to come on first but I’m happy to

00:32:54.910 --> 00:32:56.860
go.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Okay.

00:32:56.860 --> 00:33:04.680
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Yeah, so we – you know,
right at the beginning we had focused on the

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:09.500
need that our staff will have to be supported
and to access support, you know, confidentially,

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:18.080
safely, in their own times and in a multitude
of ways. And you know, the people plan had

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:22.029
put health and safety and well being, mental
health of our staff, right, at the top of

00:33:22.029 --> 00:33:26.090
the agenda but what COVID showed us is that,
actually, we need to accelerate that offer.

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:32.450
So I’ll talk first about at the national
level but there have been very, very powerful

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:36.120
interventions at local level that have supported
staff as well.

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:41.669
So at the national level, we set up with a
network of partner, wide variety of support

00:33:41.669 --> 00:33:50.410
ranging from bereavement counselling through
Hospice UK to a helpline that is free to all,

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:55.669
1.4 million people in the NHS, as well as
everybody in social care that they can call

00:33:55.669 --> 00:34:01.330
confidentially and this is with the Samaritans.
We have bespoke financial advice as people

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:11.280
were struggling with housed financial pressures.
And then recently we have also added a bespoke

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:16.210
service for colleagues from the Filipino community,
especially the nursing community who have

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:25.050
lost – you know, have borne quite a heavy
burden of loss. We have [inaudible] there

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:34.390
is some feedback, sorry. Some people, like,
who access mental health apps, so we have

00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:41.760
a range of apps available, more than 120,000[?
Pounds/hours] in May. We have a text service

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:46.460
for support as well, so people can text because
sometimes people aren’t really up to having

00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:52.340
a conversation, so they are using the text
service as well where a qualified psychologist

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:59.530
has a conversation with people and then can
sign post to specialised mental health support

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:04.240
for people who need more support than a text
conversation or a phone conversation. And

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:10.040
then of course we have one to one therapy
and counselling offered as well as part of

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:17.050
mental health support. So we are now looking
at recovery and also looking at what people

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:21.290
will need in terms of rest and taking annual
leave, etc. So we’re working with trusts

00:35:21.290 --> 00:35:26.460
and we’re going to come to [inaudible] with
recommendation on annual leave, you know,

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:31.930
I mean mandating or actively encouraging and
we’ll smooth those options out. And then

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:38.030
at the local level, people have – employers
have really stepped up to provide rest spaces,

00:35:38.030 --> 00:35:44.450
food, drinks, we also tied up with national
supermarkets who are offering – of course

00:35:44.450 --> 00:35:49.940
they offer the priority hours but some have
offered vans which are waiting in the parking

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:56.850
lot when shifts end so that people can make
their purchases right at their workplace and

00:35:56.850 --> 00:36:02.670
then go home and rest. We have of course project
wingman which had gone down extremely well

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:08.760
with the airline industry created first class
lounges to support staff. And you know, staff

00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:12.070
initially said ‘well what am I going to
do going into a lounge?’ and actually it’s

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:20.120
gone down so well because it’s a quiet space
away from the intensity of the clinical environment

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:26.140
where people who are trained in making people
feel good are having conversations, offering

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:34.850
a cup of tea or coffee or cool drink and – I
mean, non-alcoholic of course. And the airline

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:39.230
industry has said how they really valued being
a part of that support to staff.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:44.390
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Prerana. Amanda,
did you want to add to that? Okay.

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:50.070
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you. I mean, I think
Prerana’s just summed it up brilliantly.

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:56.400
I mean, I think it’s something that you
– unsurprisingly you’ll – you know,

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:02.350
we’ve spent an awful lot of time talking
about, thinking about, talking to colleagues

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:07.660
about and I know from the many, many conversations
I’ve had with colleagues up and down the

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:14.780
country, it is absolutely top of mind for
leaders – for professional leaders, for

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:20.220
managerial leaders – throughout the NHS
because there’s absolute recognition that

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:26.890
this extraordinary response from the NHS has
taken its toll and it is absolutely therefore

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:33.450
top of mind that in providing the best possible
care for patients, part of the responsibility

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:39.240
we all carry is to provide the best possible
care for our responsibility for our staff.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:44.180
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Thank you, Amanda.
I can see Joanna Shaw.

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:53.590
&gt;&gt; Joanne Shaw: Thank you David. Thanks everyone.
I guess I wanted to acknowledge amongst all

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:58.619
the extraordinary work that’s happened across
the NHS, I wanted to say something specific

00:37:58.619 --> 00:38:05.510
about the, you know, colleagues in NHSX and
NHS Digital that have facilitated this amazing

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:11.410
transformation and transition to virtual care
for so many patients in primary care and in

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:17.520
hospitals. And the speed of adoption and the
adaptation has just been, I think, so incredibly

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:24.540
impressive. I’ve also been hearing from
doctors and particularly on the hospital side

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:30.720
I sense that during this period patients have
really – many patients with long-term conditions

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:37.160
have really stepped up and engaged more with
their care than they have done in the past.

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:41.500
You know, maybe driven a bit by concern about
not wanting to end up in hospital and put

00:38:41.500 --> 00:38:47.240
further burden on the NHS and risk being infected.
And that they’re managing their medicines

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:51.600
more actively, they’ve been doing more exercise,
they’ve been adjusting their medicines with

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:54.690
help from their GPs without waiting for outpatient
appointments.

00:38:54.690 --> 00:39:00.170
So I’m sort of thinking given all the challenges
that we have in terms of capacity going forward,

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:06.790
you know, is this the moment to really build
on that sort of bedrock of face to face one

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:12.570
to one consultations with professionals online
and digitally, to step up all the other kind

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:22.240
of self and home activities that technology
can help with, that sort of self-assessment,

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:27.450
self-management, self-monitoring, self-administration.
You know, the whole sort of gamut of the kinds

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:33.660
of techniques that other industries use to
leverage the power of technology to multiply

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:38.510
the efforts of professional and to bring values
to the patients. So I wondered what your thoughts

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:43.950
are about that at this time.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Amanda, would you like to comment

00:39:43.950 --> 00:39:47.460
on that?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Let me start but I’m

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:53.650
sure other colleagues will very much want
to contribute. So I think that’s a really

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:58.750
important point Joanne, thank you. And you’re
quite right. What we’ve seen across the

00:39:58.750 --> 00:40:05.750
NHS but very much supported by colleagues
in NHSX, NHS Digital, and thank you for acknowledging

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:13.520
their extraordinary work over this period
as well has been a whole scale shift to really

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:23.660
adopting technology, absolutely now in a way
that, although we had, I think, had that ambition

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:29.850
before coronavirus, by force of circumstance,
it’s just been – the momentum has been

00:40:29.850 --> 00:40:36.520
extraordinary and the change support that
also I know has been put in place locally

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:41.020
and again supported by the national teams
to enable that to happen as well as by absolutely,

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:47.700
you know, local, there’s local clinicians,
has really led to an extraordinary set of

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:54.250
changes. So I think [inaudible] we had – I’m
sorry, I’ve got a bit of an echo. We’ve

00:40:54.250 --> 00:41:03.930
had, I think, about 46% of all outpatient
and appointments provided in a non face-to-face

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:10.240
way across the last couple of months. So you
know, that’s just a tiny snapshot. I mean,

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:14.340
that does reflect the fact that overall volumes
of outpatient appointments have gone down

00:41:14.340 --> 00:41:20.150
but nonetheless, they’ve been able to maintain
that level of access safely for patients using

00:41:20.150 --> 00:41:24.040
technology, it’s clearly something that
we need to build on and go further. We’re

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:28.720
going to talk, I think, in a moment, aren’t
we, about health and equalities as one of

00:41:28.720 --> 00:41:34.370
the big areas of focus for the NHS going forward
and certainly one of the things that I know

00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:37.510
my colleagues who’ve been working on this
have been very thoughtful about is how we

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:45.890
make sure that in using different technologies
that does not become a source of inequality

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:50.900
and actually it’s used as very much something
that is accessible to everybody regardless

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:56.690
of what kind of level of digital access they
have. And in the main, I think one of the

00:41:56.690 --> 00:42:03.390
things this has shown is that you can actually
use the telephone for a lot of these things

00:42:03.390 --> 00:42:09.340
as well as the more sophisticated types of
digital systems that we’ve got available.

00:42:09.340 --> 00:42:16.550
And so yes, absolutely, I think that this
has got to be about continuing to build on

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:21.260
the momentum that we’ve got now. It is one
of the good things that has come out of the

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:26.200
last few months. I think we’ve absolutely
got to pay attention to the need to think

00:42:26.200 --> 00:42:31.140
about your point Joanna, which is changing
the way we provide services, not just digitising

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:35.990
the way we provide services, so it’s a much
more fundamental change in the way that we

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:40.520
do things. And thirdly, I totally agree, this
is about putting people in charge of their

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:44.760
own care. And Ian may want to say a bit more
about this because I think it’s also at

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:50.510
the heart of the thinking about the NHS at
home, which is really about how we give people

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:54.620
access to the sort of technology that allows
them to monitor their own conditions and seek

00:42:54.620 --> 00:43:00.210
help when they need it, which is a very different
way of conceptualising the care offer.

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:02.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ian, would you like to comment
on that?

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:07.950
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much David and
Amanda and Joanne. And I think the point you

00:43:07.950 --> 00:43:19.150
make, Joanne, around self-management is incredibly
well made. Back in January 2019, NHS England

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:24.930
Public Board had a presentation from James
Sanderson, our Director of personalised care

00:43:24.930 --> 00:43:31.400
where we launched our programme of personalised
care and six different pillars within that.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:37.120
Since then, there’s been a huge amount of
work on care and support planning, on the

00:43:37.120 --> 00:43:43.930
work around patient activation, on driving
use of personal health budgets and social

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:48.300
prescribing. And at the heart of all of that
work is how do we end up really empowering

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:53.150
individuals and it would be great at some
stage to come back and tell the Board more

00:43:53.150 --> 00:43:57.690
about where we are on that because I think
we’re mainly ahead of trajectory.

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:06.180
James is also now leading work that Amanda
has alluded to around reinvigorating a drive

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:12.900
on telemedicine and telehealth and I was really
encouraged at the government’s whitepaper

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:17.710
Rebuilding our Future made really explicit
reference to that opportunity and what James

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:24.400
is looking to do is to learn the lessons from
previous attempts to embedded telemedicine

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:30.920
and telehealth in the NHS, for example the
health system demonstrated in 2011 that Ara

00:44:30.920 --> 00:44:38.050
was involved in and I think now is exactly
the right moment to take this opportunity.

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:41.200
So thank you for raising it.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ian. I think we

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:47.900
may well bring back the hospital at home concept
back to a subsequent board meeting. We mustn’t

00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:52.480
leave this section of the meeting without
bringing Julian in, I think.

00:44:52.480 --> 00:45:02.370
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: I’m here. What would you
like me to pick up, David?

00:45:02.370 --> 00:45:06.430
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Anything on the finance side
that you would like to bring to the Board’s

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:09.250
attention before we move on?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: I mean, the thing to bring

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:14.830
to the Board’s attention is clearly we’ve
put in place – I mean, very rapidly a whole

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:21.510
set – you know, kind of a new set of financial
rules and frameworks specifically designed

00:45:21.510 --> 00:45:31.480
to deal with very much that kind of first
emergency phase of the response. I mean, in

00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:36.320
essence to take out a lot of the sort of contracting
and commissioning work and guaranteed levels

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:42.670
of income and allow people to kind of reflect
and claim some of the additional costs we

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:46.010
knew they were going to face due to COVID
when we didn’t quite know what those were

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:50.550
going to be. We clearly now at this stage
– well, and we said at the time that would

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:57.080
run through to the end of July so we’re
clearly now at the stage of firming up the

00:45:57.080 --> 00:46:05.410
proposals for the regime that needs to exist
for the second half of the year and in discussion

00:46:05.410 --> 00:46:10.470
with the department and across government
about, you know, what those rules should be

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:15.950
and what the funding framework should be for
the balance of the year to enable us both

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:24.290
to bring in financial discipline, make sure
we are incentivising exactly the challenge

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:29.250
we’ve been discussing in the earlier part
of this meeting about, you know, getting the

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:36.280
recovery of normal health services whilst
reflecting the fact that undoubtedly, you

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:44.860
know, there are cost pressures due to just
the fact of working in a very different environment

00:46:44.860 --> 00:46:50.320
because of the – well, because of COVID
and some of the infection control methods

00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:56.620
that are required.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Julian. Before moving

00:46:56.620 --> 00:47:02.300
onto the next paper with Prerana and Ian on
the very important issue of the disproportionate

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:07.470
impact of COVID on people from BAME backgrounds
and on health and equalities [inaudible].

00:47:07.470 --> 00:47:11.340
I should just refer to Emily Lawson who’s
the only exec [inaudible] who’s probably

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:18.630
not going to get a major role at this meeting,
just to acknowledge that Emily took over the

00:47:18.630 --> 00:47:23.330
commercial role which includes procurement,
I think on March 31st, right in the middle

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:29.730
of this. Just to acknowledge Emily, what an
extraordinarily wonderful and heroic job that

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:34.170
you’ve done in that role since you’ve
took it on. But just to acknowledge that because

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:39.480
maybe at the next meeting we’ll have more
to say about procurement and supply issues.

00:47:39.480 --> 00:47:43.700
But so can we now move on to Prerana please
for your paper?

00:47:44.780 --> 00:47:52.300
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank you Chairperson. I
wanted to give some headlines from the work

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:57.741
on responding to the disproportionate impact
of COVID on black, Asian and minority and

00:47:57.741 --> 00:48:03.560
ethnic staff and health inequalities. For
the latter topic, I will be handing over to

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:09.150
my colleague Ian Dodge. On 15th April, Simon
Stevens had convened a meeting of leaders

00:48:09.150 --> 00:48:13.770
and healthcare and representative bodies such
as the BMA and Royal College of Nursing to

00:48:13.770 --> 00:48:18.290
discuss the emerging evidence and agree a
plan of action to address the impact of COVID

00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:25.690
on our BAME workforce. We then set up a series
of work streams underpinned by three principles,

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:31.490
which were about protecting and supporting
and engaging our staff. On 2nd June there

00:48:31.490 --> 00:48:36.580
was a support about Public Health England
and it published a review Disparities in the

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:41.960
Risk and Outcomes of COVID-19 and on 16th
June PHE followed up its initial descriptive

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:48.280
report with the – you know, going beyond
the data understanding the impact of COVID-19

00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:52.960
on BAME groups. And this was a summary of
its stakeholder engagement and literature

00:48:52.960 --> 00:48:58.960
review. The report puts forward the relationship
between ethnicity and health and the disproportionate

00:48:58.960 --> 00:49:04.810
impact on BAME groups and said it’s likely
to be a result of a combination of factors,

00:49:04.810 --> 00:49:09.680
including social and economic inequalities,
racism, discrimination and stigma, differing

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:14.950
risks at work and inequalities in the prevalence
of conditions such as obesity, diabetes, hypertension

00:49:14.950 --> 00:49:18.610
and asthma.
And what we had done on the 29th of April

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:24.131
before these two reports that have come out
is issued a letter asking employers to undertake

00:49:24.131 --> 00:49:30.920
risk assessments of vulnerable staff – actually,
all staff but there was – along with a clinical

00:49:30.920 --> 00:49:36.240
reference group, there was a stratification
of different staff vulnerabilities and staff

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:43.450
that were at risk. Ethnicity is a factor in
that risk stratification. So on 29th April,

00:49:43.450 --> 00:49:48.770
as a precautionary measure NHS ENI sent a
clear instruction to the system that employers

00:49:48.770 --> 00:49:54.760
need to risk assess staff with potentially
greater risk. We then also engaged with the

00:49:54.760 --> 00:50:01.380
community of HR directors and offered guidance
and support on how this risk assessment should

00:50:01.380 --> 00:50:07.340
be undertaken and what some of the mitigating
actions need to be. Best practice case studies

00:50:07.340 --> 00:50:12.050
on deploying these risk assessments were also
collected and shared both for primary care

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:16.910
and secondary care settings. We have also
created a multi-disciplinary task and finish

00:50:16.910 --> 00:50:23.350
group which is looking at FIT testing along
with the IPC cell team with Ruth May, quality

00:50:23.350 --> 00:50:29.140
improvement experts and policy leads. We are
also carefully watching the staff testing

00:50:29.140 --> 00:50:35.140
data and it shows so far that asymptomatic
staff testing demonstrates no significant

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:40.010
discrepancy in access for testing for BAME
staff groups. And this is just to say that

00:50:40.010 --> 00:50:47.050
for all of the processes that are staff facing,
we are looking at what the disproportionate

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:52.650
impact of COVID would mean. So whether it’s
staff testing, whether it’s ensuring that

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:58.670
educational processes around FIT testing and
PPE are inclusive. So we have a task and finish

00:50:58.670 --> 00:51:03.590
group set up to examine the reinforcement
of educational messages on hand hygiene, social

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:10.390
distancing and PPE to staff that may not benefit
from traditional information channels, so

00:51:10.390 --> 00:51:17.190
such as ancillary staff, agency staff, porters
and cleaners. Again, collected best practice

00:51:17.190 --> 00:51:26.240
across the system via the HR Director channel
and then have shared it on custom built dedicated

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:32.900
microsite on the NHS EI website so that employers
can access this information. So that is in

00:51:32.900 --> 00:51:39.100
the pillar of protecting staff.
Supporting staff, we have heard some concerns

00:51:39.100 --> 00:51:45.650
about staff being able to – and this is
pre-COVID but staff raising concerns and so

00:51:45.650 --> 00:51:50.610
we remain committed to a culture of openness
and transparency and we wrote out jointly

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:56.020
with the National Guardians Office and RES
votes to all the employers reinforcing our

00:51:56.020 --> 00:52:02.120
support for staff speaking out and we are
placing a lot of emphasis on recruiting BAME

00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:08.280
freedom to speak up guardians so that there
is diversity representation in the guardians

00:52:08.280 --> 00:52:13.670
as well.
We have, after following – you know, following

00:52:13.670 --> 00:52:17.920
engagement with Filipino colleagues, we launched
a tailored counselling service offered in

00:52:17.920 --> 00:52:24.690
the Tagalog speaking specialist counsellors
and of course there is the health and wellbeing

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:31.170
offer that is available to all 1.4 million
NHS staff as well as social care staff across

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:38.940
a variety of areas such as financial support,
bereavement counselling, mental health support

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:46.150
and one on one therapy as well. We also are
focussed on supporting better representation

00:52:46.150 --> 00:52:51.810
in decision making, so a joint letter was
issued by myself and Dido Harding, Chair of

00:52:51.810 --> 00:52:57.210
NHS Improvement on 19th May to ensure that
NHS organisations focus on race equality through

00:52:57.210 --> 00:53:04.270
the RES and although some reporting across
other areas was stood down, reporting on the

00:53:04.270 --> 00:53:09.630
RES and the DES, which is Disability Equality
standards, was still continued. We’re also

00:53:09.630 --> 00:53:14.670
working through our regional directors to
see how we can make some of the incident response

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:21.280
mechanisms representative and reflective of
the communities we serve. The last piece is

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:25.920
– but you know, really, really critical,
is the engaging of staff because we want to

00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:33.000
create spaces where staff can feel safe and
supported to speak out and staff networks,

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:38.880
BAME staff networks are a key vehicle of change.
So we’ve had two national webinars. The

00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:43.410
first one towards the end of April and then
six weeks later. And I want to thank members

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:51.280
of the Board who joined and heard some of
the experiences of the staff members. I want

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:58.120
to thank staff who are, you know, taking the
time and the effort to share those experiences

00:53:58.120 --> 00:54:07.660
so that we can offer better support.
Next steps, we have the race and health observatory

00:54:07.660 --> 00:54:13.260
being set up that is being hosted by the NHS
on FED[?] and will identify and tackle the

00:54:13.260 --> 00:54:19.200
specific health challenges facing people from
BAME backgrounds. We have – we’re working

00:54:19.200 --> 00:54:24.950
on the refresh of the people plan post-COVID
and looking at how we can make sure that issues

00:54:24.950 --> 00:54:31.720
that COVID has highlighted in terms of inequality
are adequately, appropriately and assertively

00:54:31.720 --> 00:54:41.890
set out in the refreshed people plan. And
I think I will stop here and say that I think

00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:47.260
that this is a moment that we’re all seeing
are the opportunity to make sure that we can

00:54:47.260 --> 00:54:51.980
continue the work. There’s a lot more to
do but we are – this is the number one issue

00:54:51.980 --> 00:54:56.860
that everybody is focussed on. And we’ve
seen in the last couple of weeks just on the

00:54:56.860 --> 00:55:03.390
very tangible measure of risk assessments
a real uptick in trust reporting 00% risk

00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:09.410
assessment for their staff via the HR directors.
So let me hand over to Ian.

00:55:09.410 --> 00:55:14.161
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, Prerana. So I’m
going to touch on health inequalities. Local

00:55:14.161 --> 00:55:20.560
systems have already been doing a lot to tackle
inequalities in healthcare delivery as part

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:27.790
of the implementation of the long term plan.
And as Simon observed earlier, COVID has now

00:55:27.790 --> 00:55:33.790
thrown a really harsh spotlight on the inequalities
that exist in our society and the inverse

00:55:33.790 --> 00:55:40.240
care law in the NHS. For example, for BAME
groups, patients in our most deprived communities

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:46.110
or who otherwise often have greatest difficulty
in accessing the healthcare they need; e.g.,

00:55:46.110 --> 00:55:51.910
people who are homeless, something that Victor,
of course, when he was with us often reminded

00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:58.960
us about. And to meet that challenge, a rapid
task group has now been established under

00:55:58.960 --> 00:56:05.890
the leadership of Owen Williams, the Chief
Executive of Calderdale and Huddersfield foundation

00:56:05.890 --> 00:56:11.540
trust who also previously served as a local
authority chief executive. And our deliberate

00:56:11.540 --> 00:56:19.080
focus is to try and generate more momentum
right now. So the exam question Owen is posing

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:26.420
is what practical changes is the NHS now going
to achieve with its partners in the next six

00:56:26.420 --> 00:56:35.600
months. And these outputs will feed into the
NHS recovery work on phase 3 that Simon and

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:41.050
Amanda have talked about and the final point
I wanted to make is really one on behalf of

00:56:41.050 --> 00:56:47.180
the whole executive team, the national directors
and the regional directors, that we, all of

00:56:47.180 --> 00:56:54.410
us, are committed to embedding a strong focus
on inequalities within our programmes, our

00:56:54.410 --> 00:57:02.060
regions, systems, local communities and neighbourhoods
as key deliverables for the NHS working with

00:57:02.060 --> 00:57:07.710
our partners. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ian. Will[?] you

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:12.150
feel very passionately about this issue and
you wrote a note to the Board setting out

00:57:12.150 --> 00:57:15.100
your views. Have you got any reflections you’d
like to share with us?

00:57:15.100 --> 00:57:20.510
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Yeah, I mean, I think that the
issue was, which I think was mentioned already,

00:57:20.510 --> 00:57:25.220
has been exposed very harshly by this crisis
and it’s a shame it’s taken the crisis

00:57:25.220 --> 00:57:32.440
to expose the issue. The important thing is
we measure and we see visible change as soon

00:57:32.440 --> 00:57:39.020
as possible, not just because of this issue
around the impact of COVID on BAME but also

00:57:39.020 --> 00:57:45.180
in thinking about the roles being put forward.
I mean, that’s one of the things Prerana

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:48.150
mentioned in her introduction. So the roles
they’ve been performing and what that exposed

00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:53.510
them to and also the issue about how people
speak up. I mean, that issue of speaking up

00:57:53.510 --> 00:58:00.990
and so the most basic requirements for safety
probably contributed to the rather high incidents

00:58:00.990 --> 00:58:08.790
of COVID deaths on BAME staff. But I’m encouraged
and I know that the Board takes this very

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:13.550
seriously and I can see that beefing up that
speaking up, particularly with BAME representatives

00:58:13.550 --> 00:58:19.890
is a path through but it’s not the only
answer and I know that in future meetings

00:58:19.890 --> 00:58:24.710
Prerana and her colleagues were coming forward
with further initiatives to make sure that

00:58:24.710 --> 00:58:28.260
this doesn’t become a flash in the pan issue,
it’s something that’s actually on our

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:33.560
agenda that we take really seriously and continue
to deliver for, you know, for this vastly

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:39.410
important segment of our workforce.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you Will[?]. I wonder

00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:43.020
David Roberts whether – you’ve talked
very passionately as well about health and

00:58:43.020 --> 00:58:46.530
equalities, whether you have anything you’d
like to add to that.

00:58:46.530 --> 00:58:53.450
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So I think that the – thank
you, David. I’ve spent the last period of

00:58:53.450 --> 00:58:59.630
time reflecting and listening and talking
to people across many different areas, such

00:58:59.630 --> 00:59:05.080
as Prerana and [inaudible] would be two great
examples of that. And I think the thing that

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:12.540
strikes me most is the environment we have
to create is where each and every one of us

00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:23.310
recognise our responsibility to call out,
tackle unacceptable behaviour at every time

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:29.660
we see it. And I’ve learned more and my
eyes have been opened talking to members of

00:59:29.660 --> 00:59:34.570
the BAME community over the last two weeks
than perhaps I thought before. So I think

00:59:34.570 --> 00:59:39.160
there’s something for all of us to learn.
The second thing is, you know, I’ve been

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:44.780
on a couple of these calls and what is absolutely
clear is actions will speak louder than words.

00:59:44.780 --> 00:59:52.340
And therefore, I think we have to be really
clear on the leadership standards we accept

00:59:52.340 --> 00:59:59.100
or not accept and be willing to address and
take actions where those standards are not

00:59:59.100 --> 01:00:03.800
met. And thirdly, I think the boards at all
level and, you know, this is a conversation

01:00:03.800 --> 01:00:12.880
on the other boards that I have the privilege
to serve on, that they take ownership of this

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:24.760
issue. And I’ve been incredibly moved by
just sort of the sheer spirit, bravery and

01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:29.340
commitment of some of the colleagues, you
know, right across the NHS but particularly

01:00:29.340 --> 01:00:35.520
some of the people from the BAME community
who I’ve spoken to who have just, despite

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:39.840
an environment that in certain places have
not been conducive, have just done the most

01:00:39.840 --> 01:00:45.820
remarkable job for patients even though it’s
not been a great environment for them personally.

01:00:45.820 --> 01:00:51.480
And we should thank and commend them but also
set out that we owe it to everyone that that

01:00:51.480 --> 01:00:54.975
is not allowed to continue.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, David. Well look,

01:00:54.975 --> 01:01:01.150
Prerana, you can tell from what both [inaudible]
and David were saying and Dido has said, I’ve

01:01:01.150 --> 01:01:06.100
said, others have said, that you’ve got
the full support of the Board behind what

01:01:06.100 --> 01:01:11.321
you’re trying to do and are going to do.
So all power to your elbow. And Ian, the health

01:01:11.321 --> 01:01:17.050
inequalities more generally is hugely important,
particularly as we move into a recession over

01:01:17.050 --> 01:01:21.890
the next year or two, you know. Absolutely
essential to what we do.

01:01:21.890 --> 01:01:26.070
Good, well thank you all very much. There’s
a noting about the use of the seal[?], which

01:01:26.070 --> 01:01:30.360
I think is just for noting. On any other business,
I wonder whether Dido, you’ve got any sort

01:01:30.360 --> 01:01:35.820
of reflection you’d like to leave us with
and then maybe Laura as well who’s, I don’t

01:01:35.820 --> 01:01:40.580
know, helping out a lot and has been over
the last couple of weeks.

01:01:40.580 --> 01:01:46.110
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: No but I should actually put
on the record to thank Laura as my Deputy

01:01:46.110 --> 01:01:52.950
for stepping up as I have been quite busy
on NHS Test and Trace. I guess my reflection

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:59.430
from the meeting, David, would be quite how
much has happened in the NHS and in the country

01:01:59.430 --> 01:02:05.010
since our last board meeting and as a number
of us have said, how humbling it is to play

01:02:05.010 --> 01:02:09.570
a very small part and to thank everyone but
also to recognise how much there is ahead

01:02:09.570 --> 01:02:16.360
of us as well and that our next board meeting
is really important decisions and sort of

